COMMAND NAME: mppstart.sh

Starts a Greenplum Database system.

*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

mppstart.sh [-d <master_data_directory>] [-B <parallel_processes>] 
            [-z] [-m] [-y] [-s] [-e] [-a] [-q] [-l <logfile_directory>] 
            [-D] [-t | -? | -v]


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The mppstart.sh script is analogous to functionality offered by the PostgreSQL 
pg_ctl and postmaster commands, which are used to start the database server. 
When you start a Greenplum Database system, you are actually starting several 
postmaster database server processes at once (the master and all of the 
segment instances). The mppstart.sh script handles the startup of the 
individual instances. Each instance is started in parallel.

Before you can start a Greenplum Database system, you must have initialized 
the system using mppcreatecluster.sh first.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-d <master_data_directory>

Optional. The master host data directory. If not specified, the value set for 
$MASTER_DATA_DIRECTORY will be used.


-B <parallel_processes>

The number of segments to start in parallel. If not specified, the script 
will start up to 60 parallel processes depending on how many segment 
instances it needs to start.


-z (force start)

Optional. Forces the startup of a segment instance with an invalid status. 
This may be desirable if the segment instance or host has a minor error that 
prevented startup in the first place. This option will allow you to start 
the segment instance (after fixing a problem) if it was marked as invalid 
in a previous startup attempt. The default behavior is to not start segment 
instances with an invalid status. If the segment instance is up and running 
then it will be marked valid again next time the Greenplum master tries to 
connect to it.


-m (master only)

Optional. Starts the master instance only, which may be useful for 
maintenance tasks.


-y (do not start standby master)

Optional. Do not start the standby master host. The default is to start the 
standby master host and synchronization process.


-s (rollover master log file)

Rollover the log file for the master instance. Moves the current logfile to 
<log file>.YYYYMMDD and starts a new log file.


-e (rollover segment log files)

Rollover the log files for the segment instances. Moves the current segment 
logfile to <log file>.YYYYMMDD and starts a new log file per segment. Not 
applicable in a master only startup.


-a (do not prompt)

Do not prompt the user for confirmation.


-q (no screen output)

Run in quiet mode. Command output is not displayed on the screen, but is still 
written to the log file.


-l <logfile_directory>

The directory to write the log file. Defaults to ~/gpAdminLogs.


-D (debug)

Sets logging level to debug.


-t (show default script settings)

Displays the settings that will be used to start the system, such as the 
master data directory and port, and the name, data directory, port, valid 
status, and primary or mirror status of all the segment instances.


-? (help)

Displays the online help.


-v (show script version)

Displays the version, status, last updated date, and check sum of this script.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Start a Greenplum Database system:

mppstart.sh

Force start of the system if it has failed segment instances from a 
previous startup attempt:

mppstart.sh -z

Start the Greenplum master instance only:

mppstart.sh -m

Display the online help for the mppstart.sh script:

mppstart.sh -?


